## 3. Sabotage A — permission-guard glob escape (`src/guards/permission-guard.ts:277`)

### A-literal (as instructed: remove `g` flag only)
- Edit: `...replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&")` → `...replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/, "\\$&")` (via edit tool, single line)
- Command: `bun test test/guards.test.ts` → EXIT 0 (42 pass, 0 fail)
- Finding: VACUOUS sabotage — `glob[index]` is a single character, so `.replace()` with/without `g` is behaviorally identical. The gate correctly stays green; this proves the literal instruction cannot red-proof the fix and a semantically effective variant is required.
- Restore: `git checkout -- src/guards/permission-guard.ts` → clean (verified via `git status --short`, empty)

### A-effective (drop escaping entirely — proves the regression test is live)
- Edit: `source += glob[index].replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&");` → `source += glob[index];`
- Command: `bun test test/guards.test.ts` → EXIT 1 (41 pass, 1 fail)
  - Failing assertion: `matchesAnyGlob > escapes every regex metacharacter so *.ts does not match ats (incomplete-sanitization regression)` at `test/guards.test.ts:170` — `expect(matchesAnyGlob("ats", ["*.ts"])).toBe(false)` received `true` (unescaped `.` compiles as any-char, exactly the pre-fix HIGH `js/incomplete-sanitization` bug class)
- Restore: `git checkout -- src/guards/permission-guard.ts` → clean; re-ran `bun test test/guards.test.ts` → EXIT 0 (42 pass, 0 fail)

